WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:07.840
Hi guys, my name is Karan Choudhary and I
was born and raised in a small town Faridabad

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:09.099
in India.

00:00:09.099 --> 00:00:14.650
Before becoming an actor I was a salesman
in a clothing store and I used to sell shoes

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:17.430
and clothes in Connaught Place, New Delhi.

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:21.340
And right after that I became a gym trainer
and I was a trainer for about three to four

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:27.350
years and after that I moved to Bombay to
pursue my career as a model and actor.

00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:33.010
And in 2013 I applied at The Neighborhood
Playhouse to study acting in New York City.

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:35.039
So I moved here in 2013.

00:00:35.039 --> 00:00:40.690
Since then I’m actively pursuing acting
and recently I started making my own projects.

00:00:40.690 --> 00:00:46.110
Last year I made a documentary (DEDICATION)
and this year I made a short film called NEW

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:47.110
YORK’D.

00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:50.180
I wrote it, produced it, directed and acted
in it.

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:55.690
NEW YORK’D has been accepted in many film
festivals and we have won about five awards

00:00:55.690 --> 00:00:58.570
in various categories.

00:00:58.570 --> 00:01:02.949
“…I have a date today and because of you,
I’m going to miss it.”

00:01:02.949 --> 00:01:04.210
Karan as Sean in NEW YORK’D

00:01:04.210 --> 00:01:05.259
[Scene to NEW YORK’D movie]

00:01:05.259 --> 00:01:06.259
[Text appears and delivery man shows Karan
the phone]

00:01:06.259 --> 00:01:07.259
“Hey, look…my boss” Kyle Mcllhone as
The Delivery Guy

00:01:07.259 --> 00:01:08.259
“Alright, I’ve also got to go.”

00:01:08.259 --> 00:01:09.259
Replies Sean

00:01:09.259 --> 00:01:10.259
“Just pay me and deal with your own personal
problems later, huh?”

00:01:10.259 --> 00:01:11.259
“My personal problems?

00:01:11.259 --> 00:01:13.490
You’re the one who is causing these personal
problems.”

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:14.490
“What?

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:17.271
Are you blaming me for getting yourself locked
out of your apartment?”

00:01:17.271 --> 00:01:18.271
“You are the delivery guy.

00:01:18.271 --> 00:01:19.271
You’ve got one job to do.

00:01:19.271 --> 00:01:20.271
If you had done your job like you are supposed
to.”

00:01:20.271 --> 00:01:22.969
“If you’d cook your own food like you’re
supposed to…”

00:01:22.969 --> 00:01:25.139
“You make a living because of people like
me.”

00:01:25.139 --> 00:01:27.649
“And you get to enjoy hot food in bed because
of people like me.”

00:01:27.649 --> 00:01:28.649
“Yeah…where is it?

00:01:28.649 --> 00:01:29.649
Where is my hot food?”

00:01:29.649 --> 00:01:33.169
“I just want to get my money and get out
of here.”

00:01:33.169 --> 00:01:34.169
“Hey Sean…”

00:01:34.169 --> 00:01:35.169
Voice from the sidewalk breaks up the conversation.

00:01:35.169 --> 00:01:37.340
Karan Choudhary: The number one problem that
I am facing (and I’m sure a lot of people

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:41.880
who come from different countries are still
facing) is having to deal with their immigration

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:42.880
status.

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:47.060
People who come from different countries and
to pursue their career as an actor in this

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:50.770
country (United States) you have to have the
Artist Visa.

00:01:50.770 --> 00:01:56.469
It’s really hard to get an Artist Visa and
even if you have the Artist Visa a lot of

00:01:56.469 --> 00:02:03.159
the production (film and TV both) would still
not give you the job because they consider

00:02:03.159 --> 00:02:07.499
people who have green cards or people who
are citizens of this country.

00:02:07.499 --> 00:02:14.070
So all that work that you put into getting
the O-1 Artist Visa, it’s almost useless.

00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:20.390
The only way to get the jobs and pursue a
successful acting career is to get a green

00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:21.390
card.

00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:26.920
And how I am facing that problem and trying
to solve it is by making my own projects.

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:30.269
The faster you can apply for the green card,
the better for you.

00:02:30.269 --> 00:02:36.439
And a way to do it is make as many projects
as you can, take them to the film festival,

00:02:36.439 --> 00:02:39.130
let’s see if they win some awards.

00:02:39.130 --> 00:02:40.680
Get press.

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:46.989
And then once you have enough projects about
yourself, then you can apply for the green

00:02:46.989 --> 00:02:51.650
card and the chances are you will be much
more successful.

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:55.189
Another problem that I’m facing is financing
for projects.

00:02:55.189 --> 00:02:56.439
I had an idea.

00:02:56.439 --> 00:02:59.950
I wrote a script and now I’m going into
production.

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:08.269
But when it comes to budgeting I was surprised
that turning a script into a film is way hard

00:03:08.269 --> 00:03:14.959
than I thought so what I did budgeting wise
that my latest film NEW YORK’D was originally

00:03:14.959 --> 00:03:18.379
written according to night night sequences.

00:03:18.379 --> 00:03:24.019
So we turned the night sequences into day
sequences so that saved me money on the lighting.

00:03:24.019 --> 00:03:29.969
There was a lot of shots of which I planned
in Time Square which which is really, really

00:03:29.969 --> 00:03:35.220
hard to get along with the equipment and crew
and especially shooting and retakes.

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:40.909
So what I did was I took all those sequences
and put it in a Greenwich Village streets,

00:03:40.909 --> 00:03:45.639
which are much easier to shoot, much nicer
to look at on camera.

00:03:45.639 --> 00:03:48.189
The other problem was the crew.

00:03:48.189 --> 00:03:54.560
So a lot of jobs were distributed to my actors,
myself (I was doing probably three or four

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:59.540
jobs on the day of shooting) so I cut a lot
of crew and that saved me a lot of money and

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:05.379
that’s how I was able to shoot NEW YORK’D
under a very limited budget and in just one

00:04:05.379 --> 00:04:06.819
day.

00:04:06.819 --> 00:04:11.639
There’s another problem I face and I’m
sure a lot of first-time filmmakers are still

00:04:11.639 --> 00:04:15.959
facing, that they have finished their first
film, how do they take it to their audience

00:04:15.959 --> 00:04:18.570
(especially to the film festivals)?

00:04:18.570 --> 00:04:24.080
If you go on Film Freeway’s site, there’s
about six to seven thousand festivals listed.

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:28.180
Each festival would cost you from $30 to $60.

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:32.949
If you decide to submit your film in 20 festivals,
that’s going to cost you a lot of money.

00:04:32.949 --> 00:04:39.479
So what I did, I wrote a personal email to
every film festival that I wanted to submit

00:04:39.479 --> 00:04:41.130
and even more.

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:47.490
I sent a personal email to more than a thousand
film festivals saying “I’m an immigrant.

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:49.130
This is my first film.

00:04:49.130 --> 00:04:52.990
And all I want is some sort of a discount
or waiver.”

00:04:52.990 --> 00:04:56.500
And you would be surprised what a success
it was.

00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:59.810
I was successful in 90% of the time.

00:04:59.810 --> 00:05:06.639
I submitted by second film NEW YORK’D in
60 film festivals and 50 of those film festivals

00:05:06.639 --> 00:05:08.720
gave me full waiver.

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:18.120
All you’ve got to do is ask because the
answer is always “no” until you ask.

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:19.930
Thank you so much for watching this video.

00:05:19.930 --> 00:05:23.750
I would love to know what kind of problems
you are facing, especially if you happen to

00:05:23.750 --> 00:05:28.290
be from a different country or any other visa
problem or if you’re an actor and trying

00:05:28.290 --> 00:05:31.520
to make your own projects and the problems
you are facing.

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:32.669
Thank you so much guys.

00:05:32.669 --> 00:05:32.939
Bye!

